Install and enable this profile
===============================


Quick path
==========

A self-contained installer is provided as ``install.sh``: it installs build
deps, builds and installs ``pam_rssh.so``, copies the profile to authselect,
and configures the sudoers env_keep entry needed for SSH agent forwarding.

    bash install.sh

The script is annotated below — read on for the manual step-by-step.
1. Install prerequisites

   * authselect
   * pam-u2f  (module and pamu2fcfg utility)
   * sssd     (if you use remote identity/auth providers)
   for pam_rssh.so:
   * pam-devel
   * openssl-devel
   * rust
   * cargo

   On Fedora/RHEL:
     sudo dnf -y install authselect pam-u2f pamu2fcfg sssd
     sudo dnf -y install pam-devel openssl-devel rust cargo

2. Copy profile files to authselect custom profiles directory

     sudo mkdir -p /etc/authselect/custom/sssd-u2f
     sudo cp -a . /etc/authselect/custom/sssd-u2f/
     git clone --recurse-submodule https://github.com/z4yx/pam_rssh.git
     cargo fix --lib -p pam_rrsh
     cargo build --release
     sudo cp target/release/libpam_rssh.so /usr/lib64/security/pam_rssh.so

3. Generate FIDO2 SSH key files

   This profile uses sshformat, so keys are generated with ssh-keygen and
   stored in ~/.ssh/. The same files work for both system auth and SSH.

   Standard keys (PIN-capable, e.g. YubiKey 5C NFC, 5C Nano):

     ssh-keygen -t ed25519-sk -O resident -O verify-required -f ~/.ssh/u2f_first
     # touch key when prompted

     ssh-keygen -t ed25519-sk -O resident -O verify-required -f ~/.ssh/u2f_second
     # touch backup key when prompted

   YubiKey Bio — early editions (fingerprint enforced by firmware, no PIN fallback):

     ssh-keygen -t ed25519-sk -O resident -f ~/.ssh/u2f_bio
     # touch key and provide fingerprint when prompted

   YubiKey Bio — newer editions (supports PIN fallback):

     ssh-keygen -t ed25519-sk -O resident -O verify-required -f ~/.ssh/u2f_bio
     # Also update the bio line in system-auth to:
     #   pam_u2f.so cue sshformat userverification=1 pinverification=0 authfile=.ssh/u2f_bio

   Keep file permissions restrictive:

     chmod 600 ~/.ssh/u2f_*

3a. Configure sudoers env_keep for SSH agent forwarding

   ``pam_rssh`` validates ``SUDO`` by signing a challenge with a key from
   the forwarded SSH agent. Sudo strips most environment variables by
   default; it must preserve ``SSH_AUTH_SOCK``:

     echo 'Defaults env_keep += "SSH_AUTH_SOCK"' | sudo tee /etc/sudoers.d/10-rssh-agent
     sudo chmod 0440 /etc/sudoers.d/10-rssh-agent
     sudo visudo -cf /etc/sudoers.d/10-rssh-agent

   Without this step, ``sudo`` will fall through pam_rssh.so and prompt for
   a password instead of asking the YubiKey to touch.

4. Select the profile

   Basic (U2F only, PIN required for both login and sudo):
     sudo authselect select custom/sssd-u2f with-pam-u2f

   U2F with touch-only sudo (PIN still required for login):
     sudo authselect select custom/sssd-u2f with-pam-u2f with-nopin4sudo

   With optional features (adjust to your system):
     sudo authselect select custom/sssd-u2f with-fingerprint with-pam-u2f with-sudo with-rssh-agent with-mdns4 with-mdns6

   Capture your existing profile options to preserve them:
     mapfile -t OPTS < <(authselect current | awk '/^- with-/ {gsub("^- ",""); print}')
     sudo authselect select custom/sssd-u2f "${OPTS[@]}" with-pam-u2f

5. Apply and verify

     sudo authselect apply-changes
     authselect current

6. Test before logging out

   Always test sudo and a new terminal login before closing your current
   session. If something goes wrong, revert with:

     sudo authselect select sssd --force

Notes
-----

* The profile does not build pam_u2f from source. Install via your
  package manager.

* The routing logic in system-auth uses paired guard+key blocks (each guard
  jumps exactly success=1 over its partner). Do not insert lines between a
  guard and its pam_u2f.so partner, and do not change the include tags on one
  without changing the other.

* `with-pam-u2f` enables the PIN/biometric path. `with-nopin4sudo` is an
  optional override that enables touch-only behavior for sudo/polkit/ssh.

* If you add a new key, add a complete guard+key pair — one for the
  touch-only (sudo) path and one for the PIN/bio (GDM) path.

* Review system-auth and password-auth before enabling on any production host.
  In particular, verify the list of login services in the guard lines matches
  the GDM PAM files present on your system:
    ls /etc/pam.d/gdm-*

Troubleshooting
===============

Sudo still asks for password despite all the above
--------------------------------------------------

If ``sudo whoami`` keeps prompting for a password instead of asking the
YubiKey to touch, the most likely cause is that the SSH agent is not
actually being forwarded into the session. ``pam_rssh`` then has nothing
to query. Two real-world traps that surface as identical symptoms:

1. **SSH client points at the wrong agent socket.**

   On Fedora the system-wide ``/etc/ssh/ssh_config.d/50-redhat.conf``
   typically sets ``IdentityAgent /run/user/<uid>/ssh-agent.socket``,
   which is a *different* socket from the one used by ``gnome-keyring``
   (``/run/user/<uid>/gcr/ssh``). Your shell's ``ssh-add -l`` shows your
   keys via ``$SSH_AUTH_SOCK``, but ``ssh`` itself will silently use the
   ``IdentityAgent`` socket — and that one is empty. ``ssh -A`` then
   forwards the empty agent.

   Diagnose::

     echo "$SSH_AUTH_SOCK"
     ssh-add -l                     # works in your shell
     ssh -G <host> | grep -i agent  # different socket?

   Fix in ``~/.ssh/config`` for the affected host (or under ``Host *``)::

     Host <host>
         IdentityAgent SSH_AUTH_SOCK

   The literal string ``SSH_AUTH_SOCK`` is a special token that tells
   ``ssh`` to use the environment variable instead of a fixed path.

2. **Stale ControlMaster multiplex.**

   If your ``~/.ssh/config`` enables ``ControlMaster auto`` (common under
   ``Host *``) and you connected once *without* ``-A``, every subsequent
   ``ssh -A <host>`` will silently join the existing master and inherit
   its (no-forwarding) state. ``ssh -O exit <host>`` does not always
   clean this up.

   Force a fresh connection::

     rm -f ~/.ssh/S.<user>@<host>:<port>
     ssh -A <host>

   Or bypass multiplex for one connection::

     ssh -A -o ControlPath=none <host>

   Adding ``ForwardAgent yes`` to the ``Host`` block in ``~/.ssh/config``
   makes future logins forward by default, so you don't depend on
   remembering ``-A``.

Verifying the chain end-to-end
------------------------------

After ``ssh -A <host>``, on the remote host::

  ssh-add -l                              # must list your keys
  sudo printenv SSH_AUTH_SOCK             # must print a path
  sudo -k && sudo whoami                  # YubiKey should blink

If ``ssh-add -l`` fails with "communication with agent failed", the
forwarded socket exists but the upstream agent is unreachable — that's
trap #1 above. If ``sudo printenv SSH_AUTH_SOCK`` is empty, ``sudoers
env_keep`` is missing — see step 3a above.
